      "writtenOn": "2025-01-03T00:21:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "nit. I\u0027m not too familiar with common usages of this... but is it necessary to have both a range (input) and data (output) offset? I\u0027m thinking that (at least since this is in C++), the `rangeOffset` could just be passed in via `values + rangeOffset` to begin with? \n\nMain reason I\u0027m asking is because having both offsets makes it a bit confusing to use and users could easily mistake one offset for the other leading to unintended behavior?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 68,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 71,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "e7e98241e0d9a22cc9214464a817b8bb64f10879",
